WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.807
You might already be using AI on
the farm without calling it that.

00:00:05.572 --> 00:00:08.905
Let's walk round the
place and pick it out.

00:00:09.355 --> 00:00:10.635
Start with the herd.

00:00:10.655 --> 00:00:14.095
Collars and ear tags track
movement, chewing and rest,

00:00:14.115 --> 00:00:15.266
day and night.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.321
Software learns what normal
looks like for each cow,

00:00:18.341 --> 00:00:20.688
and flags her when something changes.

00:00:20.942 --> 00:00:26.005
Heat, a health problem, or
her cud chewing dropping off.

00:00:26.455 --> 00:00:29.417
Virtual fencing collars
go one step further.

00:00:29.764 --> 00:00:33.784
They use GPS and a sound cue to keep
stock inside a line you draw on your

00:00:33.804 --> 00:00:34.677
phone.

00:00:35.023 --> 00:00:37.997
More dairy farms are trialling them now.

00:00:38.447 --> 00:00:43.093
In the yards, EID tags and scales
record every animal's weight.

00:00:43.346 --> 00:00:45.658
Much of that is plain automation.

00:00:45.692 --> 00:00:48.433
A reading goes in, a number comes out.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.613
The AI part is when the
software predicts something,

00:00:52.774 --> 00:00:56.909
like the day each lamb
will reach killing weight.

00:00:57.359 --> 00:01:01.716
Out on the paddocks, satellite pasture
services estimate your cover from

00:01:01.736 --> 00:01:02.725
space,

00:01:02.978 --> 00:01:05.836
paddock by paddock, every few days.

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:07.682
That's prediction,

00:01:07.936 --> 00:01:10.434
built from images and past readings.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:13.976
It can be off under cloud,
or in steep country,

00:01:13.996 --> 00:01:17.679
so a plate meter walk
still earns its place.

00:01:18.129 --> 00:01:21.556
Weather forecasts lean on
AI more each year too.

00:01:21.809 --> 00:01:26.735
The big forecasting centres now run
AI weather models alongside the older

00:01:26.755 --> 00:01:28.069
physics models.

00:01:28.415 --> 00:01:30.820
A good forecast gives you a range.

00:01:31.074 --> 00:01:33.526
Seventy percent chance of rain is a guess,

00:01:33.559 --> 00:01:35.616
with the odds written on it.

00:01:36.066 --> 00:01:39.423
What's new since about
2023 is the third kind,

00:01:39.584 --> 00:01:41.594
AI that writes and talks.

00:01:41.848 --> 00:01:44.543
It can draft an email to the stock agent,

00:01:44.797 --> 00:01:48.074
read a long consent document
and pull out the conditions,

00:01:48.094 --> 00:01:52.391
or turn your voice notes
into a tidy diary.

00:01:52.841 --> 00:01:56.316
Some tools now go a step
further and do jobs for you,

00:01:56.336 --> 00:01:58.973
like booking or filling in forms.

00:01:59.435 --> 00:02:03.547
That's early days, so watch what they do.

00:02:03.997 --> 00:02:06.856
When a rep tells you a
product has AI in it,

00:02:06.876 --> 00:02:08.608
three questions cut through.

00:02:08.769 --> 00:02:10.525
What does it sense or predict?

00:02:10.545 --> 00:02:11.732
From what data?

00:02:11.752 --> 00:02:14.239
And how often is it wrong?

00:02:14.689 --> 00:02:16.770
A good rep will have straight answers.

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:20.507
And whatever the tools say, keep
your own eyes on the stock.

00:02:21.109 --> 00:02:24.466
You know your animals and your country.

00:02:24.916 --> 00:02:27.357
Next, we'll look at
what chat AI does well,

00:02:27.377 --> 00:02:29.399
and where it gets things wrong.
